 },
 "claim": "Wires carry one atom's halo onto another atom's cut without changing the native count. On two cubes joined by a rigid strip from an 8-cycle (exact on the whole 26-point space), an atom in the far cube relays its halo across the strip into the near cube. A second atom at each of the 13 other points and lines of the two cubes meets that halo on its cut lines or ports in all 13 placements, and on its own halo lines in all 6 placed at points. Yet each removal's halo in the whole space equals its halo with its atom alone (13 of 13), and the count is 2, one piece per atom, in all 11 separated placements; the 2 touching ones merge. On a cube hub relaying into two prisms, an atom on the ring lands its halo in one receiver or, from two ring points, in both, and it meets the cut of a second atom at all 24 receiver points it reaches (each atom alone, exact; the whole space follows by the splitting lemma). The count reads the quiet glue and the lines the glue creates at, and a wire changes neither.",
